00:34:09.629 --> 00:34:11.310
PROFESSOR: But singing badly
wouldn't have the same impact

00:34:11.310 --> 00:34:12.900
if there wasn't a brass
band behind him.

00:34:15.760 --> 00:34:19.360
So you can think about how any
of these verbs-- and we've

00:34:19.360 --> 00:34:21.850
done this exercise by taking
verbs and turning them into

00:34:21.850 --> 00:34:23.550
game mechanics before.

00:34:23.550 --> 00:34:25.912
And you can think about how
any of these verbs could

00:34:25.912 --> 00:34:27.162
actually be turned
into a game.

00:34:29.580 --> 00:34:32.944
Kiss might be a goal or it might
be a rule, a mechanic,

00:34:32.944 --> 00:34:36.020
to be able to accomplish
something.

00:34:36.020 --> 00:34:37.389
Parade might be a setting.

00:34:37.389 --> 00:34:41.785
Campaign might be a collection
of mechanics that feed off

00:34:41.785 --> 00:34:42.150
each other.

00:34:42.150 --> 00:34:44.229
It could be a whole system that
tells you how successful

00:34:44.229 --> 00:34:46.340
your Save Ferris campaign
is going.

00:34:46.340 --> 00:34:47.565
You could make a game that's
just all about saving Ferris,

00:34:47.565 --> 00:34:50.570
as opposed to playing Ferris
Bueller himself.

00:34:56.020 --> 00:35:00.070
And that's kind of one exercise
that you can do when

00:35:00.070 --> 00:35:02.690
you're thinking about your
reward systems and all the

00:35:02.690 --> 00:35:04.810
systems that you guys have
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] as far as I

00:35:04.810 --> 00:35:06.050
can recall.

00:35:06.050 --> 00:35:08.860
You don't have to worry about
licensing the idea.

00:35:08.860 --> 00:35:10.880
But you're basically working
with a real live franchise.

00:35:10.880 --> 00:35:14.880
And if you're doing something
based on political diplomacy

00:35:14.880 --> 00:35:19.040
or something like that, then
there are real life source

00:35:19.040 --> 00:35:21.345
material things that you're
trying to be faithful to.

00:35:21.345 --> 00:35:22.940
And going through
this exercise--

00:35:22.940 --> 00:35:23.860
OK.

00:35:23.860 --> 00:35:26.890
What are all the verbs in this
idea that we're working with?

00:35:26.890 --> 00:35:28.110
This isn't going to work--

00:35:28.110 --> 00:35:29.710
What do people actually do?

00:35:29.710 --> 00:35:33.142
It's a good exercise to be able
to try to be faithful to

00:35:33.142 --> 00:35:34.830
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:35:34.830 --> 00:35:36.750
And then, you don't have to pick
all of them, of course.

00:35:36.750 --> 00:35:37.440
You're just going
to cherry pick.

00:35:37.440 --> 00:35:39.510
And you want to prioritize
a little bit.

00:35:39.510 --> 00:35:39.980
OK.

00:35:39.980 --> 00:35:42.990
What things can we get rid of
and very still clearly be

00:35:42.990 --> 00:35:45.340
Ferris Bueller game.

00:35:45.340 --> 00:35:47.720
Running.

00:35:47.720 --> 00:35:50.890
I think as long as we have some
kind of evation think,

00:35:50.890 --> 00:35:52.590
physical running may
not be necessary.

00:35:52.590 --> 00:35:54.780
You could do something like as
long as they're not in one

00:35:54.780 --> 00:35:56.520
location where the principal
is at any given

00:35:56.520 --> 00:35:58.332
time, you're good.

00:35:58.332 --> 00:36:00.180
But you don't have to physically
move through

00:36:00.180 --> 00:36:02.716
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] basically
in all that.

00:36:02.716 --> 00:36:03.550
AUDIENCE: Drive, probably.

00:36:03.550 --> 00:36:03.820
PROFESSOR: Driving.

00:36:03.820 --> 00:36:04.270
Yeah.

00:36:04.270 --> 00:36:06.680
It's such a minor part
of the film.

00:36:06.680 --> 00:36:07.840
Yeah.

00:36:07.840 --> 00:36:11.293
The actual driving is such a
minor part of the film that

00:36:11.293 --> 00:36:13.760
you can probably do
without that.

00:36:17.800 --> 00:36:20.430
And you might actually want to
think about what if instead of

00:36:20.430 --> 00:36:23.110
this being Ferris Bueller's day
off into Ferris Bueller's

00:36:23.110 --> 00:36:24.500
multiple days off.

00:36:24.500 --> 00:36:26.340
Maybe just play each
day differently.

00:36:26.340 --> 00:36:29.030
And just trying to stay out
more and more days.

00:36:29.030 --> 00:36:31.190
Maybe a score or number
of days you don't go

00:36:31.190 --> 00:36:33.490
to school or something.

00:36:33.490 --> 00:36:36.180
So that's an idea.

00:36:36.180 --> 00:36:40.043
That's one approach that you
can use in your projects.

00:36:43.640 --> 00:36:46.660
Before we hit two, I just want
to touch on another point.

00:36:46.660 --> 00:36:51.590
Before I transition,
any questions about

00:36:51.590 --> 00:36:53.930
dealing with IP ?

00:36:53.930 --> 00:36:56.240
There is a point that Brenda
makes in her book--

00:36:56.240 --> 00:36:57.640
the Brenda [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:36:57.640 --> 00:36:59.230
book-- that if you go
professionally in the game

00:36:59.230 --> 00:37:02.530
industry, you will most likely
at some point in time,

00:37:02.530 --> 00:37:04.495
probably many times in
your career, work

00:37:04.495 --> 00:37:06.060
with a licensed product.

00:37:06.060 --> 00:37:08.900
And it can be painful because
you do not have greater

00:37:08.900 --> 00:37:14.325
control yet trying to be
faithful to what you're

00:37:14.325 --> 00:37:14.820
working with.

00:37:14.820 --> 00:37:18.105
And if you succeed, you
make a lot of money.

00:37:18.105 --> 00:37:19.720
And maybe you get enough
money to start

00:37:19.720 --> 00:37:21.270
working on your own projects.

00:37:21.270 --> 00:37:26.390
But it's a reality because
franchises do sell.

00:37:26.390 --> 00:37:29.050
And they're a lot lower risk
than dealing with a

00:37:29.050 --> 00:37:32.660
completely new idea.

00:37:32.660 --> 00:37:34.430
By the way, if you haven't
played the new Tomb Raider

00:37:34.430 --> 00:37:37.890
game, which is actually kind
of interesting because it's

00:37:37.890 --> 00:37:39.140
like Tomb Raider meets Diablo.

00:37:41.920 --> 00:37:44.010
If people have never...

00:37:44.010 --> 00:37:47.580
Whose played or seen some one
play Tomb Raider before?

00:37:47.580 --> 00:37:48.270
OK.

00:37:48.270 --> 00:37:50.950
Who knows about this latest game
that I'm talking about.

00:37:50.950 --> 00:37:53.410
It's kind of like 3/4
isometric view.

00:37:53.410 --> 00:37:53.750
Yeah.

00:37:53.750 --> 00:37:57.660
It is Tomb Raider meets Diablo
but it still keeps in with the

00:37:57.660 --> 00:37:58.520
puzzle solving.

00:37:58.520 --> 00:37:59.350
It's cooperative.

00:37:59.350 --> 00:38:02.260
It's a very, very different
game in terms of

00:38:02.260 --> 00:38:03.380
how you play it.

00:38:03.380 --> 00:38:05.560
But it will be an interesting
exercise to sort

00:38:05.560 --> 00:38:07.950
of play that game.

00:38:07.950 --> 00:38:10.030
Maybe I'll load it
up here sometime.

00:38:10.030 --> 00:38:12.950
And you can take a look and
how does that resemble the

00:38:12.950 --> 00:38:15.545
Tomb Raider franchise so that
people who look at the game

00:38:15.545 --> 00:38:19.050
and still say, yeah that's Tomb
Raider even though it's a

00:38:19.050 --> 00:38:21.450
very new game.

00:38:21.450 --> 00:38:22.850
You can imagine like a Tomb
Raider board game, for

00:38:22.850 --> 00:38:24.950
instance, and how would
that be different.

00:38:24.950 --> 00:38:26.590
And how could that still
be faithful?

00:38:26.590 --> 00:38:28.382
That's World of Warcraft games
and Starcraft board

00:38:28.382 --> 00:38:30.482
games that we invent.

00:38:30.482 --> 00:38:33.380
[UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE]

00:38:33.380 --> 00:38:36.290
But I'd love to try it out.

00:38:36.290 --> 00:38:37.450
Let's see.

00:38:37.450 --> 00:38:39.560
So the other point I want
to get to is kind of the

00:38:39.560 --> 00:38:41.610
legalistic definition of IP.

00:38:41.610 --> 00:38:45.710
And I don't want to spend an
entire class on this because I

00:38:45.710 --> 00:38:48.350
know you will all fall asleep.

00:38:48.350 --> 00:38:50.560
I just want to point out that
there's quite a lot of

00:38:50.560 --> 00:38:51.570
different ways that

00:38:51.570 --> 00:38:53.470
intellectual property is protected.

00:38:53.470 --> 00:38:57.780
People who are creating stuff
professionally, creatively,

00:38:57.780 --> 00:39:00.650
want to make sure that they can
finish their product, they

00:39:00.650 --> 00:39:04.340
can make sequels of their
product, they can maybe even

00:39:04.340 --> 00:39:07.510
license their ideas
to someone else.

00:39:07.510 --> 00:39:11.810
Make IP for some company without
having ideas copied

00:39:11.810 --> 00:39:15.230
outright and not seeing any
compensation for that.

00:39:15.230 --> 00:39:20.030
If it is copyrights, there's
trademarks, there's patents.

00:39:20.030 --> 00:39:21.280
I feel like I'm missing
something.

00:39:23.750 --> 00:39:25.720
I guess we have those
three first.

00:39:25.720 --> 00:39:27.920
And if anything--

00:39:27.920 --> 00:39:31.440
those are definitely
the three big ones.

00:39:31.440 --> 00:39:32.350
Copyright.

00:39:32.350 --> 00:39:35.610
Copyright basically protects
an expression of an idea.

00:39:35.610 --> 00:39:37.900
The idea, itself, is
not protected.

00:39:37.900 --> 00:39:43.670
If your basic idea is in a
world where aliens are

00:39:43.670 --> 00:39:47.010
attacked by space marines,
the idea, itself, is not

00:39:47.010 --> 00:39:49.620
protected, which is a relief
because a lot of people have

00:39:49.620 --> 00:39:51.810
many games on that idea.

00:39:51.810 --> 00:39:53.140
But the actual expression is.

00:39:53.140 --> 00:39:56.035
So the actual--

00:39:56.035 --> 00:39:57.150
say that you're an artist.

00:39:57.150 --> 00:39:59.350
And you're creating concept
art based on that theme.

00:39:59.350 --> 00:40:04.740
That piece of concept art is
copyrighted to either you or

00:40:04.740 --> 00:40:07.220
if you're an employee of a
company then you've probably

00:40:07.220 --> 00:40:08.980
signed over your rights
to the company.

00:40:08.980 --> 00:40:10.930
That whatever you're creating
on their dime

00:40:10.930 --> 00:40:13.600
belongs to the company.

00:40:13.600 --> 00:40:15.090
And that's defensible
in court.

00:40:15.090 --> 00:40:16.930
It's actually an automatic
protection.

00:40:16.930 --> 00:40:21.590
But it's one of those things
where it helps to be able to

00:40:21.590 --> 00:40:23.980
document that you've actually
created something.

00:40:23.980 --> 00:40:25.820
There's a copyright office that
you can send things to.

00:40:25.820 --> 00:40:29.010
But for the most part, internal
company documentation

00:40:29.010 --> 00:40:33.050
saying, this piece of art was
created on the so and so date

00:40:33.050 --> 00:40:35.500
by so and so person.

00:40:35.500 --> 00:40:39.610
It's usually good enough to
stand up in court if there's

00:40:39.610 --> 00:40:41.710
ever a dispute.

00:40:41.710 --> 00:40:46.070
So the same thing goes
for writing.

00:40:46.070 --> 00:40:49.296
The same thing goes for
audio and music.

00:40:49.296 --> 00:40:53.270
A lot of music is protected
under copyright.

00:40:53.270 --> 00:40:55.860
So if somebody creates a piece
of music that sounds an awful

00:40:55.860 --> 00:40:58.770
lot like your piece of music.

00:40:58.770 --> 00:41:01.580
There are specific rules that
allow for that to happen.

00:41:01.580 --> 00:41:08.600
But I think, if you completely
reperform the piece, you are

00:41:08.600 --> 00:41:09.910
allowed to do that.

00:41:09.910 --> 00:41:13.190
But you may have to pay the
original composers the

00:41:13.190 --> 00:41:16.850
royalties from the music.

00:41:16.850 --> 00:41:20.460
This means that if person A
composes a song, then B

00:41:20.460 --> 00:41:29.860
performs a song and releases
an album, then C could

00:41:29.860 --> 00:41:31.900
actually reperform that song.

00:41:31.900 --> 00:41:33.860
And the only royalties goes
back to the composer.

00:41:33.860 --> 00:41:41.770
It doesn't necessarily go back
to Band B. However, Band B--

00:41:41.770 --> 00:41:44.600
it depends on really how close
they sound to each other.

00:41:44.600 --> 00:41:46.860
And, of course, if Band B
happens to be also the

00:41:46.860 --> 00:41:50.010
composers, then they're going
to get royalties and win.

00:41:50.010 --> 00:41:54.120
But you see this idea that
copyright protects the

00:41:54.120 --> 00:41:55.255
expression of an idea.

00:41:55.255 --> 00:41:58.200
The composers, at some point in
time, had to write down the

00:41:58.200 --> 00:42:00.357
music, the notes,
and the lyrics.

00:42:00.357 --> 00:42:03.310
And that is what is protected.

00:42:03.310 --> 00:42:06.656
And you can't just copy that
without entering into some

00:42:06.656 --> 00:42:09.530
sort of contract saying
that you can.

00:42:09.530 --> 00:42:12.890
We have systems like Creative
Commons for instance that

00:42:12.890 --> 00:42:16.190
basically, people who are
creating your IP say, under

00:42:16.190 --> 00:42:19.106
these conditions, I am letting
people do whatever

00:42:19.106 --> 00:42:21.062
they want with it.

00:42:21.062 --> 00:42:23.996
And there are different kinds of
Creative Commons licensing.

00:42:23.996 --> 00:42:25.463
Some of them that you do--
literally whatever

00:42:25.463 --> 00:42:26.441
you want with it.

00:42:26.441 --> 00:42:28.890
They're like open
source licenses.

00:42:28.890 --> 00:42:31.570
And in some cases, it's like
well, you can do whatever you

00:42:31.570 --> 00:42:34.170
want with it as long as it's for
non-commercial use and you

00:42:34.170 --> 00:42:37.610
have to credit the origin
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:42:37.610 --> 00:42:40.393
So that's why Creative
Commons evolved.

00:42:40.393 --> 00:42:45.323
So to enable people to freely
do stuff without necessarily

00:42:45.323 --> 00:42:48.527
having to pay and enter into
separate contracts with

00:42:48.527 --> 00:42:50.750
everybody who is creating IP.

00:42:50.750 --> 00:42:55.140
So the problem is with code.

00:42:55.140 --> 00:42:58.970
If code and game mechanics
actually for this class is

00:42:58.970 --> 00:42:59.980
something I want to consider.

00:42:59.980 --> 00:43:02.410
Because you can think
of code in two ways.

00:43:02.410 --> 00:43:04.620
One, you can think of it
as a chunk of text.

00:43:04.620 --> 00:43:08.645
It's a chunk of ASCII symbols
that you type into a

00:43:08.645 --> 00:43:11.150
development environment
and you compile it.

00:43:11.150 --> 00:43:14.260
And that's an expression
of an idea.

00:43:14.260 --> 00:43:17.130
But the other way that you can
think of it is it's a machine.

00:43:17.130 --> 00:43:19.190
It's a bunch of instructions
that you feed into a machine

00:43:19.190 --> 00:43:21.220
for it to operate in
a certain way.

00:43:21.220 --> 00:43:24.796
And those things, machines,
inventions basically, can be

00:43:24.796 --> 00:43:28.050
protected by patents.

00:43:28.050 --> 00:43:31.120
That's pretty much what the
patent system is designed for.

00:43:31.120 --> 00:43:33.600
Patent system is a lot slower
because you actually have to

00:43:33.600 --> 00:43:36.880
apply to central US Patent
Office to be able to secure

00:43:36.880 --> 00:43:39.519
those, assuming that you're
looking for patent

00:43:39.519 --> 00:43:41.610
protection in the US.

00:43:41.610 --> 00:43:43.440
Every single country, every
single region has it's own

00:43:43.440 --> 00:43:44.940
patent office and it's
own process.

00:43:44.940 --> 00:43:47.670
It's really expensive.

00:43:47.670 --> 00:43:50.700
What it does is basically give
you a temporary monopoly on

00:43:50.700 --> 00:43:52.280
that invention.

00:43:52.280 --> 00:43:56.020
At least, that's what they were
originally designed for.

00:43:56.020 --> 00:44:00.190
You make an invention, and
you've got like 25 years or

00:44:00.190 --> 00:44:05.580
something to be able to
capitalize on it before anyone

00:44:05.580 --> 00:44:06.830
can use your idea.

00:44:06.830 --> 00:44:09.090
And the whole reason why it was
invented is that you now

00:44:09.090 --> 00:44:12.270
have an incentive to be able
to come up with inventions.

00:44:12.270 --> 00:44:14.565
You might make some
profit off it.

00:44:14.565 --> 00:44:16.950
And after the patent expires--

00:44:16.950 --> 00:44:19.190
25 years or something--

00:44:19.190 --> 00:44:22.112
because you've released your
information to the patent

00:44:22.112 --> 00:44:26.150
office, now, all of society
can benefit.

00:44:26.150 --> 00:44:27.960
Can make derivatives
of your invention.

00:44:27.960 --> 00:44:32.690
So in a sense, it was invented
to eventually release great

00:44:32.690 --> 00:44:33.870
monopolies.

00:44:33.870 --> 00:44:38.120
I got this idea, and I am going
to be upfront and very

00:44:38.120 --> 00:44:40.940
clear about how I make
this thing work.

00:44:40.940 --> 00:44:42.830
Say Amazon patents 1-click.

00:44:42.830 --> 00:44:45.750
And I'm going to say for the
next 25 years, Amazon now has

00:44:45.750 --> 00:44:50.820
this lock on 1-click shipping
and purchasing, which is

00:44:50.820 --> 00:44:52.070
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] but it's
been [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:44:54.590 --> 00:45:00.150
But after 25 years, anyone
can do 1-click shipping.

00:45:00.150 --> 00:45:01.080
But that's the problem, right?

00:45:01.080 --> 00:45:02.770
25 years is a heck
of a long time.

00:45:02.770 --> 00:45:05.870
And by then, whatever is
invented is kind of obsolete.

00:45:05.870 --> 00:45:08.340
And a lot of the ideas that tend
to get approved by the

00:45:08.340 --> 00:45:11.430
patent office are pretty
damn obvious.

00:45:11.430 --> 00:45:14.770
I'm not going to discourage the
amount of work that Amazon

00:45:14.770 --> 00:45:17.240
has actually put into
1-click shipping.

00:45:17.240 --> 00:45:21.300
It's actually a really
complex process.

00:45:21.300 --> 00:45:24.700
But I am going to discourage,
say there is a patent on the

00:45:24.700 --> 00:45:27.110
giant floating arrow that
tells you where you're

00:45:27.110 --> 00:45:27.987
supposed to go next.

00:45:27.987 --> 00:45:29.360
They have a patent on this.

00:45:29.360 --> 00:45:33.600
Namco has a patent on mini games
that you play while your

00:45:33.600 --> 00:45:35.000
game is loading.

00:45:35.000 --> 00:45:39.470
That's why only Namco games
have mini games in those

00:45:39.470 --> 00:45:41.180
screens because they have
a patent on that.

00:45:41.180 --> 00:45:43.600
When it comes to game ideas
and game mechanics, I find

00:45:43.600 --> 00:45:45.360
this really annoying.

00:45:45.360 --> 00:45:51.330
It's a personal rant because I
think that's [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:45:51.330 --> 00:45:56.340
creation is that opening it up
is really, really tough.

00:45:56.340 --> 00:45:59.304
I don't really think game
mechanics fit in the

00:45:59.304 --> 00:45:59.720
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:45:59.720 --> 00:45:59.900
AUDIENCE: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:45:59.900 --> 00:46:02.220
Apple has is on all
the touching.

00:46:02.220 --> 00:46:04.511
On their gestures, a lot
of people can't get

00:46:04.511 --> 00:46:05.180
off the same thing.

00:46:05.180 --> 00:46:08.400
PROFESSOR: A lot of people have
the same issue that I

00:46:08.400 --> 00:46:10.990
have with game mechanics, with
software, in general.

00:46:10.990 --> 00:46:14.530
Software happens so quickly that
this kind of temporary

00:46:14.530 --> 00:46:20.765
monopoly basically means a real
monopoly over that idea.

00:46:20.765 --> 00:46:25.675
AUDIENCE: I thought that game
rules were not patented or

00:46:25.675 --> 00:46:27.148
covered by a copyright.

00:46:27.148 --> 00:46:28.621
I seem to remember hearing
that somewhere.

00:46:31.570 --> 00:46:35.480
And this was an issue which came
up when Scrabulous and

00:46:35.480 --> 00:46:38.340
Scrabble were at loggerheads
with one another.

00:46:38.340 --> 00:46:41.250
There's some weird technical
detail that I don't really

00:46:41.250 --> 00:46:46.207
know, but it kind of comes down
to the actual rules of

00:46:46.207 --> 00:46:50.430
Scrabble, the actual game
rules themselves

00:46:50.430 --> 00:46:51.894
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] are
not copyrightable.

00:46:51.894 --> 00:46:54.334
But somehow, the look and
feel of your game is.

00:46:57.270 --> 00:46:58.710
Do you know what the specific
details are?

00:46:58.710 --> 00:46:59.940
PROFESSOR: I don't know
the specific details.

00:46:59.940 --> 00:47:04.350
But how you outlined it is one
very clear way on how someone

00:47:04.350 --> 00:47:06.900
who's created a game can
protect a game without

00:47:06.900 --> 00:47:09.450
necessarily being protective
of the rules.

00:47:09.450 --> 00:47:13.150
And Scrabble can protect the
layout of their board.

00:47:13.150 --> 00:47:17.310
Because in order to come to
design the layout, they had to

00:47:17.310 --> 00:47:21.080
express the idea of what the
board was with graphic design.

00:47:21.080 --> 00:47:22.600
It's like here's where all the
triple word scores are.

00:47:22.600 --> 00:47:24.750
Here are where the double
word scores.

00:47:24.750 --> 00:47:26.310
That's an image.

00:47:26.310 --> 00:47:27.568
Images can be copyrighted.

00:47:27.568 --> 00:47:29.844
And that's protected under
copyrighting law, not under

00:47:29.844 --> 00:47:30.496
patent law.

00:47:30.496 --> 00:47:33.330
AUDIENCE: And I think
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] of copyrights

00:47:33.330 --> 00:47:36.470
and trademarks, it comes down
to like confusion, right?

00:47:36.470 --> 00:47:39.712
Like if someone could-- if
Scrabulous was similar enough

00:47:39.712 --> 00:47:42.936
to Scrabble, superficially,
that if someone could use

00:47:42.936 --> 00:47:43.680
their products.

00:47:43.680 --> 00:47:47.460
I think that is like a major
criteria, right?

00:47:47.460 --> 00:47:50.940
And then again, it comes down
to things like font, colors,

00:47:50.940 --> 00:47:52.820
titles, [INAUDIBLE].

00:47:52.820 --> 00:47:54.967
PROFESSOR: It's even more
specific when it comes to

00:47:54.967 --> 00:47:56.217
trademarks.

00:47:58.960 --> 00:48:06.080
If you can claim that the image
or a particular saying

00:48:06.080 --> 00:48:08.970
or something is closely
associated with your company's

00:48:08.970 --> 00:48:12.100
core brand, you can defend that
as a trademark if you

00:48:12.100 --> 00:48:14.340
registered it with the
trademark office.

00:48:14.340 --> 00:48:16.428
GAMBIT, the Singapore
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] game lab is

00:48:16.428 --> 00:48:19.780
actually a registered trademark
in Singapore.

00:48:19.780 --> 00:48:23.255
And actually, we had some
trouble registering that

00:48:23.255 --> 00:48:25.120
because well, [UNINTELLIGIBLE]
not the first company to come

00:48:25.120 --> 00:48:26.580
along with the word GAMBIT.

00:48:26.580 --> 00:48:29.970
There's actually another MIT
product called GAMBIT.

00:48:29.970 --> 00:48:33.570
It's a game theory
modeling system.

00:48:33.570 --> 00:48:36.610
So we actually had to negotiate
some fine words to

00:48:36.610 --> 00:48:39.040
be able to separate what we were
doing was not going to be

00:48:39.040 --> 00:48:41.690
confused with what
they were doing.

00:48:41.690 --> 00:48:41.920
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]?

00:48:41.920 --> 00:48:44.280
AUDIENCE: Are copyrights and
trademarks also for 25 years?

00:48:44.280 --> 00:48:46.600
Or are they different?

00:48:46.600 --> 00:48:47.850
AUDIENCE: [INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:48:51.460 --> 00:48:55.540
PROFESSOR: Copyrights last as
long as Walt Disney is dead.

00:48:55.540 --> 00:49:01.060
That is because the Disney
corp keeps lobbying the

00:49:01.060 --> 00:49:04.330
various branches of the United
States to extend copyrights so

00:49:04.330 --> 00:49:05.580
that they can lock
down Mickey.

00:49:07.840 --> 00:49:11.860
That's the easiest way to
tell exactly how long.

00:49:11.860 --> 00:49:13.010
In other words, right now,
you've got something

00:49:13.010 --> 00:49:14.770
copyrighted, it's in perpetuity
until Disney loses.

00:49:18.480 --> 00:49:20.320
This is actually really
annoying.

00:49:20.320 --> 00:49:22.820
Which means there's a whole
bunch of works, literature

00:49:22.820 --> 00:49:25.100
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:49:25.100 --> 00:49:30.190
that date back to the elite 20th
century that by all means

00:49:30.190 --> 00:49:32.285
really should be public
domain at this point.

00:49:32.285 --> 00:49:34.490
And then, they will pass
into public use.

00:49:34.490 --> 00:49:36.390
But they aren't.

00:49:36.390 --> 00:49:37.570
I know one thing for sure.

00:49:37.570 --> 00:49:40.330
H.P. Lovecraft is definitely
in public domain.

00:49:40.330 --> 00:49:44.198
That's why there are so
many games based on

00:49:44.198 --> 00:49:45.448
H.P. Lovecraft material.

00:49:47.890 --> 00:49:49.900
Whatever is based on Disney
material, has

00:49:49.900 --> 00:49:52.020
to be made by Disney.

00:49:52.020 --> 00:49:53.192
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:49:53.192 --> 00:49:54.442
of the copyright law.

00:50:04.740 --> 00:50:05.300
Images.

00:50:05.300 --> 00:50:10.300
If the Scrabble board is
identified as being key to the

00:50:10.300 --> 00:50:12.240
Scrabble brand, you
might be able to

00:50:12.240 --> 00:50:14.570
protect it under trademark.

00:50:14.570 --> 00:50:18.900
And trademark is one of those
things where all you're saying

00:50:18.900 --> 00:50:20.660
is that this is how our company

00:50:20.660 --> 00:50:22.410
is identifying ourselves.

00:50:22.410 --> 00:50:27.700
And if that is confused with
somebody else's product, that

00:50:27.700 --> 00:50:28.760
product is now hurting
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:50:28.760 --> 00:50:30.700
to make money.

00:50:30.700 --> 00:50:33.350
So there are a lot of steps to
allow you to fight that.

00:50:33.350 --> 00:50:34.950
The problem with trademark
is that you have to

00:50:34.950 --> 00:50:36.810
keep fighting back.

00:50:36.810 --> 00:50:40.300
So Xerox, for instance,
constantly fights to keep

00:50:40.300 --> 00:50:43.090
Xerox as a trademark because
people keep using the word,

00:50:43.090 --> 00:50:47.100
Xerox, to mean photocopy, which
is a great problem for a

00:50:47.100 --> 00:50:47.950
company to have.

00:50:47.950 --> 00:50:49.670
But it's actually a cash
drain as well.

00:50:49.670 --> 00:50:55.690
Because everyone's associating
your company's name--

00:50:55.690 --> 00:50:57.960
you're thinking Google, right?

00:50:57.960 --> 00:50:59.170
AUDIENCE: I think
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:50:59.170 --> 00:51:01.510
Too many people or lose it.

00:51:01.510 --> 00:51:02.450
Band-aid would for sure.

00:51:02.450 --> 00:51:03.860
PROFESSOR: So you have
to fight it.

00:51:03.860 --> 00:51:04.420
AUDIENCE: And Kleenex, yeah.

00:51:04.420 --> 00:51:06.300
PROFESSOR: As long as you keep
fighting it, you stand a

00:51:06.300 --> 00:51:07.790
chance of hanging onto it.

00:51:11.610 --> 00:51:13.660
Like [UNINTELLIGIBLE]
you blink.

00:51:13.660 --> 00:51:15.600
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:51:15.600 --> 00:51:17.540
AUDIENCE: I remember hearing
about this, so I was looking

00:51:17.540 --> 00:51:18.025
to find it.

00:51:18.025 --> 00:51:22.830
But Richard Garfield, who
created Magic, has a patent on

00:51:22.830 --> 00:51:26.380
methods of play for trading
card games.

00:51:26.380 --> 00:51:26.470
PROFESSOR: Like the

00:51:26.470 --> 00:51:27.030
[UNINTELLIGIBLE], for instance?

00:51:27.030 --> 00:51:29.925
AUDIENCE: And the one who owns
the patent is playing a card

00:51:29.925 --> 00:51:32.850
in a certain orientation and
then changing it's orientation

00:51:32.850 --> 00:51:36.650
in the game field choosing to
buy a different state of that

00:51:36.650 --> 00:51:38.075
character's embodiment.

00:51:38.075 --> 00:51:39.040
AUDIENCE: What?

00:51:39.040 --> 00:51:39.690
[LAUGHTER]

00:51:39.690 --> 00:51:40.997
AUDIENCE: [INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:51:40.997 --> 00:51:43.799
AUDIENCE: That is stupid.

00:51:43.799 --> 00:51:45.200
AUDIENCE: He has a patent
on tapping cards?

00:51:45.200 --> 00:51:46.440
AUDIENCE: He has a patent on
tapping cards, among many

00:51:46.440 --> 00:51:47.600
other things that
are basically--

00:51:47.600 --> 00:51:50.676
So I suspect that he doesn't
enforce this patent or that it

00:51:50.676 --> 00:51:52.950
is not enforceable because if
you try to enforce it, it gets

00:51:52.950 --> 00:51:54.240
[UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE].

00:51:54.240 --> 00:51:56.705
PROFESSOR: It is possible, but
it's not [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:51:56.705 --> 00:51:58.160
AUDIENCE: I heard
that the actual

00:51:58.160 --> 00:52:01.070
manifestation of that is that--

00:52:01.070 --> 00:52:03.010
I don't know whether
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] contested

00:52:03.010 --> 00:52:03.980
people over it.

00:52:03.980 --> 00:52:07.030
But definitely, they're
anti anyone else

00:52:07.030 --> 00:52:08.570
using the term tap.

00:52:08.570 --> 00:52:11.730
So which is why whenever you
go to look at Legend of the

00:52:11.730 --> 00:52:14.428
Five Rings or whatever, they
have the turned sideways

00:52:14.428 --> 00:52:14.900
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:52:14.900 --> 00:52:17.530
But it's called bowing.

00:52:17.530 --> 00:52:20.545
And the same is true in any
collectible card game that has

00:52:20.545 --> 00:52:23.667
a tap mechanic, they're not
allowed to use the word, tap.

00:52:23.667 --> 00:52:25.575
AUDIENCE: But that's
a copyright.

00:52:25.575 --> 00:52:27.483
AUDIENCE: Yeah, it's
a copyright.

00:52:27.483 --> 00:52:29.200
AUDIENCE: [INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:52:29.200 --> 00:52:31.938
AUDIENCE: So I don't know it's
been tested because I don't

00:52:31.938 --> 00:52:33.720
know if that patent has
been contested.

00:52:33.720 --> 00:52:34.270
AUDIENCE: I would suspect not.

00:52:34.270 --> 00:52:34.990
AUDIENCE: Probably not.

00:52:34.990 --> 00:52:37.120
But it's interesting.

00:52:37.120 --> 00:52:40.290
If you look at any of the
[UNINTELLIGIBLE], so

00:52:40.290 --> 00:52:40.682
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:52:40.682 --> 00:52:43.450
Pokemon and I don't recall
what the others are.

00:52:43.450 --> 00:52:45.570
They all use the term tap.

00:52:45.570 --> 00:52:49.810
But because of the copyright
in which they hold, they're

00:52:49.810 --> 00:52:50.890
allowed to do it, but
nobody else--

00:52:50.890 --> 00:52:51.020
AUDIENCE: [INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:52:51.020 --> 00:52:53.470
PROFESSOR: The copyright
violation probably gives

00:52:53.470 --> 00:52:56.240
evidence for a patent
violation.

00:52:56.240 --> 00:53:00.200
So if you can stay clear of the
copyright violation, the

00:53:00.200 --> 00:53:04.030
case that Wizards has to be
able to contest the same

00:53:04.030 --> 00:53:06.290
mechanic that's under
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:53:06.290 --> 00:53:07.890
But they might still
have a case.

00:53:07.890 --> 00:53:11.052
AUDIENCE: I suspect that they
think that their patent is not

00:53:11.052 --> 00:53:11.990
enforceable.

00:53:11.990 --> 00:53:12.710
PROFESSOR: It's possible.

00:53:12.710 --> 00:53:14.752
AUDIENCE: Otherwise, you
wouldn't even be able to get

00:53:14.752 --> 00:53:16.429
away with not even calling
it tapping.

00:53:16.429 --> 00:53:18.235
As long as you turn a card,
you'd be able to say, hey, do

00:53:18.235 --> 00:53:18.850
you have a method?

00:53:18.850 --> 00:53:19.650
AUDIENCE: It's so ridiculous.

00:53:19.650 --> 00:53:22.380
AUDIENCE: Entering
[UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE]

00:53:22.380 --> 00:53:26.850
the first orientation at
the player's option.

00:53:26.850 --> 00:53:28.880
Turning the trick card
another orientation

00:53:28.880 --> 00:53:29.900
on the playing surface.

00:53:29.900 --> 00:53:32.440
It doesn't even say
for any reason.

00:53:32.440 --> 00:53:33.960
AUDIENCE: In Naval Battles,
you have to turn the card

00:53:33.960 --> 00:53:35.320
upside down.

00:53:35.320 --> 00:53:39.488
Like submarine cards, you can
put them upside down to

00:53:39.488 --> 00:53:41.710
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:53:41.710 --> 00:53:44.634
AUDIENCE: [INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:53:44.634 --> 00:53:46.520
AUDIENCE: --method of playing
games when two or more players

00:53:46.520 --> 00:53:47.780
blah, blah, blah.

00:53:47.780 --> 00:53:49.430
AUDIENCE: [INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:53:49.430 --> 00:53:51.390
PROFESSOR: So you can see how
this gets annoying quick.

00:53:51.390 --> 00:53:54.870
But there's actually an,
unfortunately, good reason why

00:53:54.870 --> 00:53:57.440
things like game mechanics and
more often implementation of

00:53:57.440 --> 00:54:00.930
game mechanisms code are
protected by patents.

00:54:00.930 --> 00:54:05.490
Is in that because there are
already so many out there, if

00:54:05.490 --> 00:54:08.725
your company is just trying to
make a game, the likelihood

00:54:08.725 --> 00:54:10.786
that you're going to run into a
pile of patents is actually

00:54:10.786 --> 00:54:12.690
pretty high.

00:54:12.690 --> 00:54:14.560
So what do you do?

00:54:14.560 --> 00:54:18.670
If you've got something that you
know that you created, a

00:54:18.670 --> 00:54:21.200
lot of companies just
go and patent that.

00:54:21.200 --> 00:54:24.773
In the event that it turns out
to be a case that you run

00:54:24.773 --> 00:54:27.490
afoul of a different patent,
they might actually look at

00:54:27.490 --> 00:54:30.430
the company that's suing them
and say, but you were running

00:54:30.430 --> 00:54:33.870
to file our patents because we
just also patented something

00:54:33.870 --> 00:54:36.270
pretty obvious.

00:54:36.270 --> 00:54:39.130
EA, Activision what?

00:54:39.130 --> 00:54:41.020
AUDIENCE: It's all just mutually
assured destruction.

00:54:41.020 --> 00:54:41.450
PROFESSOR: Exactly.

00:54:41.450 --> 00:54:42.660
I mean, well, it's
not destruction.

00:54:42.660 --> 00:54:45.000
It's what they do is they
trade licenses.

00:54:45.000 --> 00:54:48.300
It's like we will let you use
these patents if you will let

00:54:48.300 --> 00:54:51.415
us use that patent and thus,
avoid a legal fight.

00:54:51.415 --> 00:54:52.080
Final costs.

00:54:52.080 --> 00:54:53.372
Lawyers are getting paid

00:54:53.372 --> 00:54:54.505
throughout this entire process.

00:54:54.505 --> 00:54:56.450
AUDIENCE: [LAUGHTER]

00:54:56.450 --> 00:54:57.446
PROFESSOR: It's cheaper
than actually

00:54:57.446 --> 00:54:58.840
bringing it to court because--

00:54:58.840 --> 00:55:02.535
AUDIENCE: Your patent isn't
validated if you can be shown

00:55:02.535 --> 00:55:04.062
someone else came up with
it and patented

00:55:04.062 --> 00:55:05.570
it earlier in time.

00:55:05.570 --> 00:55:08.110
You have to be the first person
to come up with it.

00:55:08.110 --> 00:55:09.360
AUDIENCE: [INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:55:12.550 --> 00:55:14.750
AUDIENCE: And that's why I'm
sure that Wizard has never

00:55:14.750 --> 00:55:17.530
tried to use their patent
against people because they

00:55:17.530 --> 00:55:20.070
suspect that it's not going to
hold up [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:55:20.070 --> 00:55:20.570
PROFESSOR: It's possible.

00:55:20.570 --> 00:55:21.620
You may not want to fight it.

00:55:21.620 --> 00:55:23.890
But sometimes, the threat
of a lawsuit is

00:55:23.890 --> 00:55:25.410
a big enough threat.

00:55:25.410 --> 00:55:27.705
It's like you don't even have
to be able to win it.

00:55:27.705 --> 00:55:29.740
You just-- we are
a big company.

00:55:29.740 --> 00:55:30.720
We have lawyers.

00:55:30.720 --> 00:55:31.634
And we can pay them.

00:55:31.634 --> 00:55:32.884
Do you?

00:55:34.840 --> 00:55:38.090
So that's one reason I mean
why companies that are

00:55:38.090 --> 00:55:41.463
otherwise opposed to legal
patents will still secure

00:55:41.463 --> 00:55:44.640
patents to protect themselves.

00:55:44.640 --> 00:55:45.470
It's kind of sad.

00:55:45.470 --> 00:55:47.790
It's like one of those things
where in a city where everyone

00:55:47.790 --> 00:55:50.594
owns a gun, you want
to own one, too.

00:55:53.300 --> 00:55:54.450
So that's patents.

00:55:54.450 --> 00:55:55.000
We talked about trademarks.

00:55:55.000 --> 00:55:57.180
We talked about copyright.

00:55:57.180 --> 00:56:00.130
One last thing I kind of want
to talk about is fair use.

00:56:00.130 --> 00:56:04.280
Fair use is kind of the defense
that a lot of schools

00:56:04.280 --> 00:56:05.000
fall back on.

00:56:05.000 --> 00:56:07.175
The idea that if you're doing
something, or if you're

00:56:07.175 --> 00:56:11.515
appropriating some sort of
material, or some sort of IP

00:56:11.515 --> 00:56:17.820
for political or educational
reasons, you have a defense.

00:56:17.820 --> 00:56:21.688
Should the case be brought to
court, you might be able to

00:56:21.688 --> 00:56:24.410
defend yourself and win that
case by saying well, this was

00:56:24.410 --> 00:56:24.730
the reason.

00:56:24.730 --> 00:56:26.500
So am I doing something for
satirical purposes.

00:56:26.500 --> 00:56:30.850
Am I doing something
to teach people

00:56:30.850 --> 00:56:34.130
about games, for instance.

00:56:34.130 --> 00:56:36.385
I'm going to reprint
rules or something.

00:56:36.385 --> 00:56:38.630
And you guys can look at it.

00:56:38.630 --> 00:56:40.000
And you guys can sue me.

00:56:40.000 --> 00:56:41.910
And I can claim fair use.

00:56:41.910 --> 00:56:42.800
I may not win.

00:56:42.800 --> 00:56:43.430
That's the problem.

00:56:43.430 --> 00:56:45.130
Fair use is only a defense.

00:56:45.130 --> 00:56:50.130
Fair use is not a law that
automatically protects you

00:56:50.130 --> 00:56:52.330
from being taken to court.

00:56:52.330 --> 00:56:53.750
So lawyer's fees.

00:56:57.920 --> 00:57:01.580
Who pays the lawyers' fees
depends on the case.

00:57:01.580 --> 00:57:04.710
But frankly, I would rather not
be put in that position.

00:57:04.710 --> 00:57:05.960
So there is that chilling
effect.

00:57:09.100 --> 00:57:13.160
Just keep that in mind of the
games you guys are making for

00:57:13.160 --> 00:57:14.300
the class assignments.

00:57:14.300 --> 00:57:18.000
But, of course, we don't expect
you to be say, copying

00:57:18.000 --> 00:57:20.570
text verbatim from
other rule sets.

00:57:20.570 --> 00:57:23.635
In fact, I doubt any of you
would be particularly

00:57:23.635 --> 00:57:25.730
interested in doing so.

00:57:25.730 --> 00:57:28.560
Certainly not copying art.

00:57:28.560 --> 00:57:31.120
But also, keep in mind that also
applies for things like

00:57:31.120 --> 00:57:34.420
art that you find online.

00:57:34.420 --> 00:57:35.305
You could protect yourself
under fair use.

00:57:35.305 --> 00:57:38.180
This is for a class assignment,
darnit.

00:57:38.180 --> 00:57:40.720
And for the most part, no one
is really going to notice.

00:57:40.720 --> 00:57:42.750
But, of course, if you want to
take these games a little bit

00:57:42.750 --> 00:57:45.410
further, maybe sell them in
the future, you'll want to

00:57:45.410 --> 00:57:47.620
rethink about every single piece
of art, every single

00:57:47.620 --> 00:57:49.720
piece of text that you're
using in your games.

00:57:49.720 --> 00:57:52.870
Is that coming from somewhere
else or can we claim that we

00:57:52.870 --> 00:57:53.365
invented that?

00:57:53.365 --> 00:57:56.824
AUDIENCE: Have any past students
or groups tried to

00:57:56.824 --> 00:57:58.130
pitch their games anywhere?

00:57:58.130 --> 00:58:01.080
PROFESSOR: Oh, you mean like
turn it commercial?

00:58:01.080 --> 00:58:02.520
Not to my knowledge.

00:58:02.520 --> 00:58:04.440
AUDIENCE: [INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:58:04.440 --> 00:58:04.930
PROFESSOR: Yeah, do it.

00:58:04.930 --> 00:58:09.260
I mean, based on the games that
we played on Tuesday,

00:58:09.260 --> 00:58:10.510
absolutely.

00:58:14.080 --> 00:58:16.160
Actually, one of the pieces of
feedback that we got from the

00:58:16.160 --> 00:58:17.790
GAMBIT [UNINTELLIGIBLE] every
single one of those games has

00:58:17.790 --> 00:58:19.835
something they have not seen
before in a card game.

00:58:22.690 --> 00:58:24.480
Every one of those
projects had a

00:58:24.480 --> 00:58:25.910
really interesting mechanic.

00:58:25.910 --> 00:58:27.260
And as far as I can
tell, they've

00:58:27.260 --> 00:58:28.510
all really been tested.

00:58:31.450 --> 00:58:33.035
All right.

00:58:33.035 --> 00:58:33.510
OK.

00:58:33.510 --> 00:58:37.040
So I'm not going anymore
into IP discussions.

00:58:37.040 --> 00:58:40.015
I spend too much time talking
to IP lawyers because we

00:58:40.015 --> 00:58:40.790
create IP in this lab.

00:58:40.790 --> 00:58:42.390
We create games.

00:58:42.390 --> 00:58:43.800
And if anyone happens
to be interested.

00:58:43.800 --> 00:58:46.790
I was considering
a career in law.

00:58:46.790 --> 00:58:50.420
I'll be happy to go into
greater detail.

00:58:50.420 --> 00:58:54.270
But for now, anymore
questions?

00:58:54.270 --> 00:58:54.570
OK.

00:58:54.570 --> 00:58:55.820
Let's just play games.